«
    @ RngRel: how two bounded intervals X and Y sit on the line, as one of the
    @ thirteen cases of Allen's interval algebra, which IEEE 1788-2015 names the
    @ overlap state: before, meets, overlaps, starts, containedBy, finishes,
    @ equals, finishedBy, contains, startedBy, overlappedBy, metBy, after,
    @ returned as a sentence: "X overlaps Y".
    @ X and Y may be a…b, a±b, a±p% or a plain number, with compatible units;
    @ Y is expressed in the unit of X. A value a±σb is refused: its bars are not
    @ bounds, and σConcord is the comparison that fits it.
    @ Point intervals take the first rule that matches, in the order below.
    @ After Jean Wilson's Concord (2025).

    @ Bounds of a value without unit: V → lo hi
    «
        DUP TypeName → RrK
        «
            CASE
                RrK "range" == THEN Object→ END
                RrK "drange" == THEN Object→ → RrA RrH « RrA RrH - RrA RrH + » END
                RrK "prange" == THEN
                    Object→ → RrA RrP « RrA ABS RrP × 100 ÷ → RrH « RrA RrH - RrA RrH + » »
                END
                RrK "uncertain" == THEN
                    DROP "RngRel: a±σb has no bounds, use σConcord" DoError
                END
                RrK "integer" == RrK "neg_integer" == OR RrK "decimal" == OR
                RrK "neg_decimal" == OR RrK "fraction" == OR RrK "neg_fraction" == OR
                RrK "bignum" == OR RrK "neg_bignum" == OR THEN DUP END
                DROP "RngRel: X and Y must be intervals or numbers" DoError
            END
        »
    »
    → RrBounds
    «
        → RrX RrY
        «
            @ Y in the unit of X, then both without unit
            IF RrX TypeName "unit" == THEN
                IF RrY TypeName "unit" ≠ THEN "RngRel: X has a unit, Y has none" DoError END
                RrY RrX Convert UVAL RrX UVAL
            ELSE
                IF RrY TypeName "unit" == THEN "RngRel: Y has a unit, X has none" DoError END
                RrY RrX
            END
            RrBounds ROT RrBounds
            → RrA1 RrA2 RrB1 RrB2
            «
                CASE
                    RrA1 RrB1 = RrA2 RrB2 = AND THEN "equals" END
                    RrA2 RrB1 < THEN "before" END
                    RrB2 RrA1 < THEN "after" END
                    RrA1 RrB1 = THEN IF RrA2 RrB2 < THEN "starts" ELSE "startedBy" END END
                    RrA2 RrB2 = THEN IF RrA1 RrB1 > THEN "finishes" ELSE "finishedBy" END END
                    RrA2 RrB1 = THEN "meets" END
                    RrA1 RrB2 = THEN "metBy" END
                    RrA1 RrB1 > RrA2 RrB2 < AND THEN "containedBy" END
                    RrA1 RrB1 < RrA2 RrB2 > AND THEN "contains" END
                    RrA1 RrB1 < THEN "overlaps" END
                    "overlappedBy"
                END
                @ Said as a sentence, so that the direction is plain: X overlaps Y
                "X " SWAP + " Y" +
            »
        »
    »
»
